    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Theme overview",
        "body": "Simple indicative tenses name a time without haber: present (canto), imperfect (cantaba), preterite (canté), future (cantaré), conditional (cantaría).\n\nEach ending sits on the same stem idea: you change the tip to move the action in time. Example with cantar (I): canto / cantaba / canté / cantaré / cantaría.\n\nPráctica: Conjugate one -ar, one -er, and one -ir verb in I-form for the five simple tenses. Say out loud which time each form shows.\n\nError común: Mixing imperfect and preterite (using cantaba when the action is a finished event → canté)."
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Point 1 overview",
        "body": "Simple indicative tenses name a time without haber: present (canto), imperfect (cantaba), preterite (canté), future (cantaré), conditional (cantaría).\n\nEach ending sits on the same stem idea: you change the tip to move the action in time. Example with cantar (I): canto / cantaba / canté / cantaré / cantaría.\n\nPráctica: Conjugate one -ar, one -er, and one -ir verb in I-form for the five simple tenses. Say out loud which time each form shows.\n\nError común: Mixing imperfect and preterite (using cantaba when the action is a finished event → canté)."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "Point 2 overview",
        "body": "A compound tense uses two parts: an auxiliary verb (usually forms of haber) plus a past participle (often ending in -ado / -ido). The participle stays the same; the auxiliary changes for the person.\n\nExamples: he cantado (I have sung), había comido (I had eaten), habrá vivido (will have lived). Think: helper verb + done-form of the main verb.\n\nPráctica: Write five compound forms with the same participle (cantado or comido) using he / había / hube / habré / habría. Circle the auxiliary and underline the participle.\n\nError común: Changing the participle by person (hemos comidos). The participle does not take plural -s here."
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "heading": "Point 3 overview",
        "body": "Choose simple vs compound by asking: is the action seen as finished with a link to now (compound), or placed in a clear past/future slot (simple)?\n\nContrast: «Ayer terminé» (simple preterite, closed yesterday) frente a «Ya he terminado» (present perfect, result still matters). Background habits use imperfect (leía).\n\nPráctica: Rewrite three sentences changing only the tense and explain in one line why you chose simple or compound.\n\nError común: Using present perfect with a closed «ayer» time when your variety expects simple preterite."
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "heading": "Point 1 synthesis",
        "body": "Simple indicative tenses name a time without haber: present (canto), imperfect (cantaba), preterite (canté), future (cantaré), conditional (cantaría).\n\nEach ending sits on the same stem idea: you change the tip to move the action in time. Example with cantar (I): canto / cantaba / canté / cantaré / cantaría.\n\nPráctica: Conjugate one -ar, one -er, and one -ir verb in I-form for the five simple tenses. Say out loud which time each form shows.\n\nError común: Mixing imperfect and preterite (using cantaba when the action is a finished event → canté)."
      }
    ],
